WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:06.131
The DGX Spark has 128 gigabytes of memory.

2
00:00:06.410 --> 00:00:09.045
The MacBook Pro gives a model about 46.

3
00:00:09.324 --> 00:00:11.251
Yet on the same model, the Mac streams

4
00:00:11.298 --> 00:00:12.749
about twice as fast.

5
00:00:13.649 --> 00:00:14.845
First, will it fit?

6
00:00:15.228 --> 00:00:18.409
Multiply billions of parameters by bits per weight,

7
00:00:18.583 --> 00:00:19.767
then divide by eight.

8
00:00:20.371 --> 00:00:23.216
An 8-billion-parameter model at 4 bits is 4

9
00:00:23.285 --> 00:00:24.423
gigabytes of weights.

10
00:00:24.864 --> 00:00:26.861
Add 10 to 20 percent for the KV

11
00:00:27.012 --> 00:00:30.321
cache and runtime, and plan for about 5.

12
00:00:31.321 --> 00:00:33.179
That's where memory size matters.

13
00:00:33.504 --> 00:00:37.312
A 120-billion-parameter model needs about 65 gigabytes.

14
00:00:37.521 --> 00:00:38.566
It fits the Spark.

15
00:00:38.682 --> 00:00:40.098
It doesn't fit the Mac.

16
00:00:41.198 --> 00:00:42.417
Speed is different.

17
00:00:42.661 --> 00:00:44.785
Every token the model writes reads all of

18
00:00:44.832 --> 00:00:45.877
its weights once.

19
00:00:46.086 --> 00:00:49.116
So the ceiling is bandwidth divided by size.

20
00:00:49.499 --> 00:00:53.238
The Spark reads 273 gigabytes per second: about

21
00:00:53.296 --> 00:00:55.084
55 tokens per second.

22
00:00:55.362 --> 00:00:58.404
The Mac reads 546: about 109.

23
00:00:58.915 --> 00:01:01.957
Real runs land lower, but the ratio holds.

24
00:01:03.257 --> 00:01:03.919
Your turn.

25
00:01:04.244 --> 00:01:07.553
A 70-billion-parameter model at 4 bits takes about

26
00:01:07.622 --> 00:01:08.690
40 gigabytes.

27
00:01:09.016 --> 00:01:11.175
Pause and estimate: does it fit on the

28
00:01:11.221 --> 00:01:13.845
Mac, and what's its tokens-per-second ceiling?
